/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Component: Ammonium bicarbonate (NH4HCO3).  CAS 1066-33-7.
  THE CURATION DEBT of this case, and the one demonstration it still owes.

  NEVER FED.  This solid APPEARS if the NH3 + CO2 loading crosses saturation.
  A component the student never wrote can therefore enter the answer, which
  is the point: the phase set is a RESULT, not an input.

  WHY THIS FILE REFUSES.  Both of its species are already in the network --
  NH4+ from the ammonia set, HCO3- from the carbonate set -- so the solid's
  equilibrium is fully determined by ONE more number.  That number is not
  curated, and neither route to it is available:

    * the SOLID FORMATION DATUM (which would give the equilibrium to the
      precision of the calorimetry) is not in the catalogue;
    * the MEASURED solubility product is available only as an order of
      magnitude read off the ~220 g/L (25 C) solubility -- logK25 ~ 0.25,
      which is a full log unit of uncertainty on a solid whose appearance
      or absence changes the whole answer.

  So the case REFUSES, by name, with the remedy stated.  It does not quietly
  run on the order-of-magnitude number, and it does not silently drop the
  solid.  Compare CaCO3.dat, where every constant is measured to 0.01 log
  units: the authority for an equilibrium is a property of the DATA available
  for THAT reaction, never of the reaction's category.

  Curation remedy, either one:
    (a) curate the solid formation datum (dHf_298, s_298, referenceState
        pureSolid) from a primary source, and the equilibrium follows; or
    (b) curate a measured solubility product with its primary citation, and
        declare it in solidPhases below.

  THE SEARCH, RUN 2026-07-28, so the next curator does not repeat it.  Four
  routes were tried; none closes today, and the reasons differ:

    1. A PUBLIC-DOMAIN DATABASE.  The USGS PHREEQC ammonia database (Amm.dat,
       fetched and read in full) carries the aqueous ammonia species and every
       common carbonate mineral, and NO ammonium-carbonate solid at all --
       not NH4HCO3, not teschemacherite.  Neither do the other shipped
       databases.  This is the route that would have been cleanest, and it is
       simply empty.

    2. THE SOLID FORMATION DATUM (remedy (a)).  Every route the open web
       offers ends at NBS/NIST, CRC or DIPPR -- exactly the sources this
       project excludes on LICENCE grounds (no grant, nothing to honour;
       CLAUDE.md, the CODE-vs-DATA split).  Not "hard to find": found, and
       not usable.  A licence-clean primary (CODATA Key Values is the pattern
       CaCO3.dat follows) does not cover this substance.

    3. A REGRESSED SOLUBILITY PRODUCT (remedy (b)).  It exists -- the
       NH3-CO2-H2O solid-liquid equilibrium is parameterised in the Extended
       UNIQUAC line (Thomsen & Rasmussen, Chem. Eng. Sci. 54 (1999) 1787, and
       the chilled-ammonia work after it), which explicitly carries solid
       NH4HCO3.  Two obstacles: the papers are paywalled here, and -- the
       serious one -- a Ksp regressed jointly with Extended UNIQUAC is NOT
       PORTABLE to this case's Davies ions.  The theory guide states the rule
       (ch:cryst-ksp): "a solubility product and its activity-coefficient
       model are an inseparable pair".  Importing that number under Davies
       would be a licence-clean way of being wrong.

    4. THE MEASURED SOLUBILITY, converted here.  ~220 g/L at 25 C is
       m_sat ~ 2.8 mol/kg, so I ~ 2.8 -- five times past where Davies is
       trustworthy (~0.5) and four times past where it even warns (~0.7).
       The conversion needs the very gamma the case cannot compute at that
       concentration.  This is why the estimate below carries a log unit of
       uncertainty: the number is not badly measured, the MODEL is absent.

  So the debt is now precisely stated: it is not a missing measurement.  It is
  one licence-clean thermodynamic datum (route 2) OR a Pitzer/HMW
  parameterisation for the NH4+/HCO3- pair, which would make route 4's
  conversion honest and route 3's number portable.  Either is a curation
  campaign, not a lookup, and this file stays as it is until one lands.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        NH4HCO3;
formula     NH4HCO3;
CAS         1066-33-7;

MW          79.056;            // kg/kmol
// NO `role` KEY.  Role is a property of the (component, phase) PAIR, not of
// the substance, and a design record is where the new form is written -- the
// record-form gate refuses it here and only counts it on the legacy corpus.
//
// Worth knowing if this component is ever put back into a case basis: with no
// role it defaults to `volatile`, and the engine then stops on a MISSING
// VAPOUR PRESSURE -- a generic coverage complaint that MASKS the refusal this
// file exists to raise.  The fix is the (component, phase) role migration,
// not a key here (2026-07-27).

solid
{
    rho_p   1580.0;            // particle density [kg/m^3] -- INTERIM (CRC)
}

// NO standardThermochemistry BLOCK.  Not an omission: the datum is not
// curated, and writing a placeholder would be worse than refusing.  The
// engine names this file and this field when it stops.

solidPhases
{
    ammoniumBicarbonate
    {
        dissolutionReaction { masters ( { ion NH4; nu 1; } { ion HCO3; nu 1; } ); }
        equilibrium { status curationRequired; requires ( logK25 ); note "order-of-magnitude estimate logK25 ~ 0.25 from the ~220 g/L (25 C) solubility is NOT stored: a value with a log unit of uncertainty, on a solid whose presence changes the phase set, is not a value.  The uncertainty is the MODEL, not the measurement -- m_sat ~ 2.8 mol/kg is five times past Davies.  Two remedies, both campaigns: a licence-clean solid formation datum, or Pitzer/HMW parameters for the NH4-HCO3 pair (which would also make the Extended-UNIQUAC literature Ksp portable).  Searched 2026-07-28, see the header."; }
    }
}

reviewStatus interim;
reviewReason ( primarySourceMissing );
reviewOwner  IST;
